Now I understand the vulnerability. In `readstat_convert`, when `converter` is NULL (no iconv converter), it does:
```c
} else if (src_len + 1 > dst_len) {
    return READSTAT_ERROR_CONVERT_LONG_STRING;
} else {
    memcpy(dst, src, src_len);
    dst[src_len] = '\0';
}
```

The check `src_len + 1 > dst_len` protects against overflow. But wait, the crash is a heap-buffer-overflow READ 1 in `readstat_convert`. Let me think about the source of the read overflow.

Actually, the crash is a READ overflow. In `readstat_convert`, the read happens at `src[src_len-1]` in the while loop stripping spaces, and in `memcpy(dst, src, src_len)`. The read overflow would be reading past the end of `src`.

In `sav_process_row`, `readstat_convert` is called with `src = ctx->raw_string` and `src_len = raw_str_used`. The `raw_string` buffer is allocated with `ctx->raw_string_len = longest_string + sizeof(SAV_EIGHT_SPACES)-2`.

Let me look at how `raw_str_used` is computed. In `sav_process_row`:
```c
if (raw_str_used + 8 <= ctx->raw_string_len) {
    if (raw_str_is_utf8) {
        char c;
        for (int i=0; i<8; i++)
            if ((c = buffer[data_offset+i]))
                ctx->raw_string[raw_str_used++] = c;
    } else {
        memcpy(ctx->raw_string + raw_str_used, &buffer[data_offset], 8);
        raw_str_used += 8;
    }
}
```

So `raw_str_used` is bounded by `raw_string_len`. But then `readstat_convert` is called with `src_len = raw_str_used`. The read overflow in `readstat_convert` reads `src[src_len-1]` which is within bounds. Hmm.

Wait, let me reconsider. The crash is a READ 1 overflow. Let me look at the git history / the regression range to understand what changed. Let me check the git log.
